{
  "image_name": "5761d2c673809106eb6df3c43f91a4ba.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how electronic states are constructed in a twisted bilayer or superlattice using the moiré Brillouin zone. The top sketch shows two slightly rotated Brillouin zones, drawn in blue and orange, whose mismatch creates a much smaller hexagonal moiré zone with high-symmetry points such as Γ, K, and M. A reference momentum in the original layer is related to nearby shifted momenta in the two layers.\n\nThe central panels explain the coupling procedure. Starting from an initial state \\(k_0\\), reciprocal lattice vectors from each layer generate translated copies of the state. These states are then “folded” back toward a chosen moiré corner \\(K_i\\), so their momenta are measured relative to the nearest moiré-zone point rather than the large original zones. Visually, the blue and orange point lattices represent the two layers’ momentum grids, while arrows and dashed lines indicate how interlayer tunneling connects them.\n\nThe final panel summarizes the continuum-model idea: many momentum-space states from both layers are included, but only some are strongly connected by the tunneling amplitude \\(\\tilde t(k)\\). The figure emphasizes that the twisted geometry reorganizes the two layers’ momentum states into coupled moiré minibands.",
